      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--warn); background:rgba(240,96,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--danger);">⚠ Tier 3 — Unapproved Research Chemical, Elevated Safety Concern</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Melanotan II is sold online as a "tanning peptide," is not FDA-approved for any use, and has <strong>published case reports linking it to melanoma, dangerous mole changes,
            priapism requiring surgery, and other serious adverse events.</strong> Of everything
            covered on this site, this is the compound with the clearest documented real-world
            harm signal. This page describes what's known — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance,
            and not a recommendation to use it under any circumstances.</strong> Talk to a doctor,
            and see a dermatologist about any mole changes, immediately if you've used it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Melanotan II is a synthetic analog of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone
            (α-MSH), developed originally as a potential sunless-tanning and libido agent. It
            works by non-selectively activating melanocortin receptors, which stimulates
            melanocytes (the skin cells that also give rise to melanoma) to produce more melanin —
            the same pigment-producing pathway implicated in skin cancer biology. It's sold online
            as an unregulated "research chemical," explicitly labeled not for human consumption.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Research Actually Exists</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            There is no completed large-scale human trial establishing safety for Melanotan II's
            marketed uses. What does exist is a growing body of published case reports directly
            documenting harm: melanoma diagnosed shortly after self-injected use (including in a
            20-year-old woman after just 3–4 weeks of use combined with tanning-bed exposure),
            rapid eruption of new or changing moles that can mask early skin-cancer detection,
            priapism requiring surgical intervention, rhabdomyolysis, renal infarction, and
            posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (a serious neurological condition).
            Establishing direct causation from case reports alone has real limits — people using
            Melanotan II for tanning often also have high UV exposure independently — but this is
            documented human harm, not a theoretical concern, and the Skin Cancer Foundation and
            multiple national regulators have issued explicit public warnings about it.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.skincancer.org/press/the-skin-cancer-foundation-issues-warning-regarding-melanotan-ii/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Skin Cancer Foundation — Warning Regarding Melanotan II →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Legal &amp; Regulatory Status</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Melanotan II has never been FDA-approved for any use, and its safety has not been
            established. It was among the peptides the FDA removed from its Category 2
            "significant safety risk" compounding list effective April 22, 2026 — a technical
            change in nomination status, not a safety clearance — and it does not appear on the
            FDA advisory committee's list of peptides being considered for the actual
            compounding-permitted (Category 1) list. In practice, given its melanoma and other
            safety signals, Melanotan II is expected to remain effectively restricted from
            legitimate compounding-pharmacy access regardless of its Category 2 paperwork status.
            It is sold today only through unregulated online sellers, with no purity or dosing
            oversight.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

